        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 text-primary">The Breaking Discovery</h2>

        <p class="mb-6">
          Within hours of Grokipedia's launch on October 27, 2025, users and journalists began discovering that many articles appeared to be direct copies of Wikipedia entries. What initially seemed like coincidence quickly revealed itself as a systematic plagiarism operation affecting thousands of articles.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3 mt-8">First Revelations</h3>
        <p class="mb-4">
          The first major reports came from technology journalists who noticed suspicious similarities between Grokipedia and Wikipedia content. <strong>The Verge</strong> was among the first outlets to publish detailed comparisons showing near-verbatim copying.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-red-50 dark:bg-red-950/20 p-6 rounded-lg my-6 border border-red-200 dark:border-red-800">
          <h4 class="font-semibold mb-3 text-red-800 dark:text-red-200">Key Finding: Word-for-Word Copying</h4>
          <p class="text-sm mb-2">Journalists discovered that entire sections of Grokipedia articles were copied word-for-word from Wikipedia, including:</p>
          <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-sm mt-2">
            <li>Introduction paragraphs and key facts</li>
            <li>Historical timelines and chronologies</li>
            <li>Technical specifications and data tables</li>
            <li>Even typographical errors from Wikipedia were replicated</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3 mt-8">Scale of the Plagiarism</h3>
        <p class="mb-4">
          Subsequent investigations by <strong>Wired</strong>, <strong>The Register</strong>, and <strong>Futurism</strong> revealed the staggering scope of the copying operation:
